    <!-- Detailed step body text -->
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_corner_measure">Measure %1$s from the end of the pipe to where you want the outside of the corner. That\'s your mark. (Take-off for %3$s EMT is %2$s.)</string>
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_corner_mark">Make a clear mark at %1$s from the end of the pipe.</string>
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_corner_place">Place the bender arrow tip directly on your mark. The bender heel faces the long run (the pipe going back toward where it came from).</string>
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_corner_bend">Bend to 90°. Keep the pipe flat on the floor — this is a horizontal corner, so there\'s no need to lift it.</string>
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_corner_verify">Hold the pipe up against the wall. The corner should land right where you measured. Both legs should sit flat and parallel to their respective walls.</string>
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_90_measure">Subtract the take-off (%2$s for %3$s EMT) from your desired stub height. Your mark goes at %1$s from the end of the pipe.</string>
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_90_mark">Measure %1$s from the end of the pipe and make a clear pencil mark all the way around the conduit.</string>
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_90_place">Place the bender arrow (▶) directly on your mark with the hook toward the short end. The conduit should lay flat on the floor or work surface.</string>
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_90_bend">Step firmly on the bender foot and pull the handle up until the bender reads 90° or the conduit lines up with the 90° hash mark on the bender.</string>
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_90_verify">Lay the bent conduit against a wall. Measure from the floor to the top of the stub — it should match your original target. If it\'s short, a slight back-bend is needed; if it\'s long, you over-bent.</string>
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_offset_measure">You need a %1$d° offset. Measure the exact height of the obstruction (from pipe centreline to pipe centreline).</string>
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_offset_mark">From your reference starting point, make Mark 1. Then measure %1$s further along the pipe and make Mark 2. These are your two bend centres.</string>
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_offset_first_bend">Place the bender arrow on Mark 1. Bend to %1$d°. The pipe should rise over the obstacle.</string>
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_offset_second_bend">Rotate the pipe 180° (keeping it in the same plane). Place the arrow on Mark 2. Bend to %1$d°. The pipe should now run parallel to where it started.</string>
    <string name="conduit_bend_step_offset_shrinkage">Your total run is now %1$s shorter than a straight piece would be. Account for this when cutting or planning your total length.</string>

